      "text_raw": "The use of more bits for the\nquantization also increases\nthe maximum SNR.\nIn this graph M stands for\nmultibit;\n– M4 thus stands for 4 bit\nquantization,\n– C211 stands for 4th-order\nnoise shaping with a\n2–1–1 Mash or cascaded\ntopology; note that a 2–2\nMash topology offers\nless SNR,\n– SL4 stand for a 4th-order\nsingle-loop topology.\nIt is clear that a 4-bit 4th-\norder noise shaping is by far the best.\nIt is also clear that 3rd-order noise shaping provides lower SNR values. This is true for both\na 2–1 Mash topology (C21) and for a 3rd order single-loop topology (SL3).",
      "text": "The use of more bits for the quantization also increases the maximum SNR. In this graph M stands for multibit; – M4 thus stands for 4 bit quantization, – C211 stands for 4th-order noise shaping with a 2–1–1 Mash or cascaded topology; note that a 2–2 Mash topology offers less SNR, – SL4 stand for a 4th-order single-loop topology. It is clear that a 4-bit 4thorder noise shaping is by far the best. It is also clear that 3rd-order noise shaping provides lower SNR values. This is true for both a 2–1 Mash topology (C21) and for a 3rd order single-loop topology (SL3)."
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 632,
      "printed_page": 643,
      "bbox_pt": [
        0,
        112,
        595,
        172.29857499999991
      ],
      "text_raw": "From this it can be concluded that multibit Sigma-delta realizations offer the highest SNR\nvalues for low OSR values. If matching problems have to be avoided however, single-bit solutions\nare quite attractive provided 4th order noise shaping is used in a 2–1–1 Mash topology.",
      "text": "From this it can be concluded that multibit Sigma-delta realizations offer the highest SNR values for low OSR values. If matching problems have to be avoided however, single-bit solutions are quite attractive provided 4th order noise shaping is used in a 2–1–1 Mash topology."
    }
  ],
